sections.body:
Every PPP contract ends. It ends either at **expiry**, the planned end date, or through **early termination**. Early termination is truly a **last resort**. It follows a whole range of processes that start with an act of **default** by one of the parties or a **continuing force majeure** event. High-level principles for default, force majeure, early termination and compensation may be set in the PPP legal and regulatory framework and then applied in each contract.

From a contract management view, the aim is to **avoid termination**. The team does this by:
- managing performance well;
- identifying and mitigating risks that could lead to default;
- dealing with defaults **in good time** and in line with the contract;
- managing disputes through the **Dispute Resolution Process (DRP)**.

This lesson builds on the earlier lessons about monitoring, performance points, step-in and fiscal risk. Those tools are what keep a troubled project away from termination.

The **exit strategy** is based on the contract's provisions on termination and expiry. It shows that the authority can bring the project to an efficient end and keep services running, either **in-house** or by **outsourcing** again. It should include:
- an **analysis of options** for continuing the service after termination or expiry, with a recommended option;
- plans for a **post-implementation review** of key deliverables, VfM, quality and innovation, carried out **within six months** of expiry or termination;
- steps to build the **lessons learned** into the authority's daily work;
- an **implementation plan** based on the contract's handback procedures;
- plans for any **employee transfers** from the Project Company to the authority or a successor;
- an estimate of the **resources and staff** the authority will need to manage the exit;
- a **closure event** to celebrate the project's achievements and prepare contract management staff for their new roles.

Some contracts give the authority an **option to extend**. This should be treated as just **one of the options** in the analysis. The authority should not extend by default without checking whether it offers better value than the alternatives.

The exit strategy should be reviewed at suitable points during the contract and revised so that **robust plans are in place three years before expiry**.

The 2021 EPEC guide stresses early preparation for handback. Mature market experience suggests planning should begin **five to seven years before expiry**. Key actions include enforcing maintenance obligations, ensuring final payments are made, reviewing the project scope against current strategy, planning a smooth transfer of asset management with little service disruption, and gathering information for a final evaluation.

**Agreeing the handback condition.** The expected condition of the assets at expiry must be agreed and written into the contract. At **signature**, the parties must agree which assets the authority will need at the end. Where the authority needs them, the contract should provide for transfer of possession and **unencumbered title** at expiry.

**The incentive problem.** In the last years, the Project Company has an incentive to **cut maintenance**, because money saved boosts returns. To prevent this, the contract must set a **procedure before expiry** to check asset condition and compliance. It usually involves a **survey** to:
1. examine the assets;
2. list every item needing remediation;
3. develop a remediation programme;
4. estimate the costs;
5. inspect that remediation is properly completed.

**Security.** It is common to require the Project Company to give the authority **acceptable security** for handback, as set in the contract. One approach is to retain a **percentage of yearly revenues or payments** in a reserve used for any investment needed to meet the handback condition.

**Residual life.** The assets must have **some remaining useful life**. The aim is not that the authority can use them forever, but that it does not have to **replace all assets at the same time** on expiry. For example, toll road projects have required the road to be usable for **at least three years** after expiry, and a hospital project might require equipment to have at least **one-third of its original useful life** remaining on average.
